// batch ae: Tsushima, Marne, Tannenberg, Galípoli, Verdun, Somme
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "couraçado": "The strongest warship of its day: a hull with thick armor plating and enormous guns that fired many kilometers. In 1905, the strength of a navy was measured in battleships.",
  "cruzador": "A warship faster and lighter than the battleship, with less armor. It was used to scout, escort and hunt smaller ships.",
  "destróier": "A small, very fast warship built to launch torpedoes and to protect the fleet from enemy torpedo boats.",
  "torpedeiro": "A small, fast boat that carries torpedoes. It attacks by surprise, at close range, above all at night.",
  "torpedo": "A cigar-shaped projectile that travels underwater to the enemy ship's hull and explodes. A single torpedo could sink a large ship.",
  "cruzar o t": "A naval maneuver in which a fleet in line passes across the front of the enemy line. All the guns of the first side fire, while the second can answer only with its bow guns. A T is drawn on the water.",
  "mina naval": "An explosive anchored or floating at sea, which goes off when a ship hits it or passes close by.",
  "nó": "A unit of ship speed: one nautical mile (1.85 km) per hour. Nine knots is a little over 16 km/h.",
  "esquadra": "A group of warships under a single admiral.",
  "espoleta": "The part that makes a shell explode. If it is slow or too insensitive, the projectile passes through the target without doing damage.",
  "plano schlieffen": "The German war plan against France and Russia: defeat France in about six weeks, passing through Belgium and encircling the French army, and only then turn to Russia.",
  "plano xvii": "The French plan of 1914, which expected the main German attack in Alsace and Lorraine and ordered the French to attack to recover those regions.",
  "estado-maior": "The group of officers who plan, pass on orders and handle the supply of an army. The chief of the general staff was the commander's brain.",
  "exército de campanha": "In 1914, the large formation of several hundred thousand men, with its own command, made up of several army corps. Germany had eight in the west; France, five.",
  "força expedicionária britânica": "The small professional army that Great Britain sent to France in August 1914, with about 130,000 men. In English, the BEF.",
  "reconhecimento aéreo": "An airplane flight to see where the enemy is. In 1914, it was the first war in which airplanes were used for this.",
  "linhas interiores": "When an army is in the center and the enemy around it, and the railways and roads link its parts by shorter routes. It lets troops be sent from one side to the other faster than the opponent can.",
  "telégrafo sem fio": "Sending messages by radio waves, without wires. It made it possible to warn ships and whole fleets from afar, as the Japanese ship that spotted the Russian fleet did.",
  "mensagem em claro": "A message sent without a code, which anyone who listens in can read.",
  "lagos da masúria": "A region of lakes, forests and swamps in the south of East Prussia, today in northern Poland. It made it hard for armies to march and kept them apart.",
  "dardanelos": "The strait between Europe and Asia, in Turkey, that links the Aegean Sea to the Sea of Marmara and, beyond it, to Constantinople (today Istanbul). Its narrowest point is little more than 1 km wide.",
  "anzac": "Acronym for Australian and New Zealand Army Corps, the army corps of Australians and New Zealanders. At Gallipoli, their landing place came to be called Anzac Cove.",
  "desembarque": "The operation of taking soldiers from ship to an enemy beach, in small boats, under fire.",
  "disenteria": "An intestinal disease caused by contaminated water and food, with severe diarrhea. It killed and took many soldiers out of the fighting in wars before antibiotics.",
  "guerra de atrito": "A war in which each side tries to win by killing more soldiers than it loses, until the other can bear no more, instead of seeking a quick victory.",
  "forte": "A structure of concrete and steel, partly underground, with guns and shelter for soldiers. The forts of Verdun formed a ring around the city.",
  "grupo de exércitos": "A union of several armies under a single commander. In 1916, Pétain came to command one, the Army Group Center.",
  "metralhadora": "A weapon that fires hundreds of rounds a minute, in bursts. In 1914, a single one, well placed, could stop hundreds of soldiers advancing on foot.",
  "arame farpado": "Steel wire with barbs, stretched in several rows in front of the trench. It snags advancing soldiers and leaves them stuck under fire.",
  "barragem rolante": "A curtain of artillery fire that moves forward a few meters per minute, just ahead of the infantry, so that the enemy keeps their heads down while the soldiers close in.",
  "tanque": "An armored tracked vehicle with weapons, able to cross trenches and barbed wire. It was first used in combat in September 1916.",
  "mina subterrânea": "A tunnel dug under the enemy trenches and filled with explosives. In the First World War, mines were blown seconds before an attack.",
  "batalhões pals": "British battalions made up of volunteers from the same town, factory or club, who enlisted together and served together. In English, Pals, meaning 'friends'. A heavy loss struck a whole street at once.",
  "linha hindenburg": "A heavily fortified German defensive system, built in the winter of 1916 and 1917, further behind the Somme line. The Germans fell back to it in March 1917."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  tsushima: {
    war: "Russo-Japanese War (1904–1905)",
    hook: "A Russian fleet took seven months to sail halfway around the world and was destroyed in little more than a day.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "At the start of the 20th century, Russia and Japan were competing for Manchuria and Korea. On 8 February 1904, Japan made a surprise attack on the Russian Pacific fleet at Port Arthur, and the war began. Over the following months that fleet was destroyed piece by piece: the [[battleship|couraçado]] Petropavlovsk, flagship of Admiral Makarov, hit a [[naval mine|mina naval]] and sank in April; Admiral Vitgeft died in the Battle of the Yellow Sea, in August; the last ships were sunk inside the harbor, in December. Port Arthur surrendered on 2 January 1905.",
        "With no fleet in the Pacific, Russia made an enormous gamble. The tsar sent the Baltic Sea fleet, renamed the 2nd and 3rd Pacific Squadrons, around the world to reach Vladivostok, the main Russian port left in the region. Many officers were against it. The tsar, Admiral Rozhestvensky and the Navy Minister, Avellan, decided to go ahead anyway.",
        { h: "Around the world" },
        { timeline: [
          ["8 Feb 1904", "Japan makes a surprise attack on the Russian fleet at Port Arthur."],
          ["10 Aug 1904", "Battle of the Yellow Sea: the Russian admiral Vitgeft dies, and the Port Arthur fleet falls apart."],
          ["Oct 1904", "The Baltic fleet sets out under Rozhestvensky. Near the Dogger Bank, in the North Sea, it fires on British fishing boats that it mistakes for Japanese torpedo boats."],
          ["2 Jan 1905", "Port Arthur falls. The fleet Russia was sending to help no longer had anywhere to go."],
          ["Feb 1905", "The second part of the fleet, under Admiral Nebogatov, sets out from the Baltic."],
          ["27 and 28 May 1905", "Battle of Tsushima."]
        ]},
        "The voyage was a punishment. The Suez Canal would not let the newest battleships through, and the fleet split up at Tangier and only came together again in Madagascar. The German company Hamburg-Amerika refused to supply it with coal beyond French Indochina. And while still in the North Sea, in October 1904, the Russians fired on British fishing boats they took for Japanese torpedo boats: one boat sank and six were damaged. Russia accepted the blame and paid compensation. By the time it reached Asia, the fleet had hulls fouled with growth and the crews were disheartened."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Heihachiro Togo, Japanese admiral" },
        "He commanded Japan's Combined Fleet. He had already defeated, or killed, five of the Russian admirals who had earlier commanded the Pacific fleet. He knew that if the Baltic fleet reached Vladivostok, the war at sea would start again. So his task was to destroy it before that, and he had combat experience, [[radio|telégrafo sem fio]] and newer ships to do it.",
        { h: "Zinovy Rozhestvensky, Russian admiral" },
        "He commanded the whole voyage. He had slow ships, tired crews and trouble getting coal. At Tsushima, a shell splinter hit him in the head and knocked him unconscious. Later he was taken to a destroyer, and ended up a prisoner of the Japanese.",
        { h: "Nikolai Nebogatov, Russian admiral" },
        "He brought the 3rd Squadron from the Baltic, with older ships. He took command at night, when Rozhestvensky was out of action, and the next morning surrendered what was left of the fleet. He was sentenced to death, a penalty later changed to ten years in prison, and was released in 1909."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "According to Wikipedia, Japan had 4 battleships, 29 [[cruisers|cruzador]], 21 [[destroyers|destróier]], 45 [[torpedo boats|torpedeiro]], 4 gunboats and 22 auxiliary ships. The Russians had 8 battleships, 3 smaller battleships, for coastal defense, 9 cruisers, 9 destroyers and 9 auxiliaries: 38 ships, according to the same text. On paper, the Russians had more battleships. In practice, the Japanese were better at the things that decide a naval battle.",
        "They were faster: the Japanese battleships made more than 18 [[knots|nó]], while the Russians went at about 9, at the pace of their slowest ships. They fired farther, up to 14 km against 11 km for the Russian guns, and faster: about one shot per minute, against one every two and a half minutes. The Japanese shells, with an explosive called shimose, went off on touching the target and wrecked the upper parts of ships. The Russian ones were made to pierce armor and had [[fuzes|espoleta]] that were slow to act. They often passed through the target without damage.",
        { h: "The route" },
        "To reach Vladivostok, the Russians could choose among three paths. They chose the shortest, the Tsushima Strait, between Japan and Korea. They sailed in radio silence and far from the usual routes, and the path only became clear on 25 May, when they separated the cargo ships and sent them to Shanghai, a neutral city.",
        { h: "27 May: the meeting" },
        "At 2:45, the Japanese auxiliary ship Shinano Maru saw the lights of the Russian hospital ship Orel. At 4:55 she sent a radio warning, and Togo received the message at 5:05. At 6:34 Togo sent his telegram, which ends with the sentence that became famous in Japan: 'The weather today is fine, but the waves are high.' Fog hid the fleets, but the radios kept the Japanese informed. At 1:40 p.m., the two sides sighted each other. Around 2 p.m., Togo hoisted the Z flag on the mast, which by prearrangement meant that the fate of the Empire depended on that battle.",
        { h: "The battle in moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "The Russians came in a column, and Togo went to meet it. The maneuver that followed is the most discussed of the battle: he turned the entire line of ships, one after another, in a 180-degree turn, and ended up on a course parallel to the Russians', in front of the head of the column. During the turn, the Japanese ships were exposed: at 2:08 p.m., the Mikasa, Togo's ship, was hit at 7,000 m and took about 15 hits in five minutes. The Japanese only answered at 6,400 m. But when the turn was over, the Russians at the front could use only their bow guns.",
        "The Japanese concentrated their fire on the Knyaz Suvorov, Rozhestvensky's flagship. The Oslyabya, some 90 minutes after the start, was the first to sink, and Wikipedia says it was the first modern battleship sunk by gunfire alone. The Suvorov became a wreck, and Rozhestvensky lost consciousness. The Borodino exploded after a hit on her ammunition magazine, probably from the Fuji, and only one man survived. The Imperator Alexander III was also lost. By the end of the afternoon, four Russian battleships had sunk, and the Japanese had suffered only light damage.",
        { h: "The night" },
        "Around 8 p.m., Togo released 21 destroyers and 45 torpedo boats, which attacked for about three hours. The Russians scattered, and the searchlights they switched on to find the attackers showed where they were. The Navarin hit mines linked by chains, took four torpedoes and sank: of her 622 crew, three survived. The Sissoi Veliky was hit in the stern and scuttled by her own crew the next day. The Admiral Nakhimov was hit in the bow, and the Vladimir Monomakh collided with a Japanese destroyer. Both were scuttled by their crews in the morning. The Japanese lost three torpedo boats.",
        { h: "28 May: the surrender" },
        "At 5:23, the Japanese sighted what was left of the Russian fleet, heading northeast. Nebogatov had no way to match Togo's speed or range. He hoisted the international signal of surrender and ordered the St. Andrew's flag lowered and the Japanese one raised. The Japanese, who did not have the word 'surrender' in their signal book, kept firing until they understood that the signal asked the ships to stop their engines. Only then did Togo order a cease-fire. Four Russian battleships were captured. The cruiser Izumrud escaped. Rozhestvensky was taken aboard the destroyer Byedoviy, which was trying to reach Vladivostok."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Of the 11 Russian battleships, 7 sank and 4 were captured. In addition, 14 more ships sank, and others were interned in neutral ports or disarmed. Only three warships reached Vladivostok. Between 4,800 and 5,000 Russians died, and about 6,000 were taken prisoner. The Japanese lost 117 men, had 583 wounded and lost three torpedo boats.",
        "Russia, which still had its army, asked for peace after losing almost all its heavy ships. The Treaty of Portsmouth was signed in September 1905, with the mediation of the United States.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the first time, in the modern era, that an Asian power defeated a European one in a war. Leaders such as Gandhi, Atatürk, Sun Yat-sen and Nehru celebrated the defeat of a colonial power. For Russia, the blow was enormous: it shook the prestige of the Romanov dynasty and was followed by mutinies in the navy. The Russian fleet was reduced to a level a little above that of Austria-Hungary.",
        "For the navies of the world, Tsushima showed that fast ships with big, long-range guns beat ships with guns of mixed calibers. The British admiral Fisher drew from it the lesson that big guns and speed counted, and HMS Dreadnought, which would give its name to an era, began to be built in October 1905.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "'Crossing the T': Wikipedia describes Togo's maneuver as a 180-degree turn, at the end of which the fleets were on parallel courses, and credits one account with the 'crossed T' at around 2:45 p.m. Other sources say Togo crossed the T at about 7,500 yards. The accounts do not agree, and the maneuver, which is usually taught as the classic example, is a subject of debate among historians." },
        { note: "The Z flag: the message it stood for appears in different wordings ('The fate of the Empire depends on the result of this battle' is the most common). The Togo Shrine in Tokyo says it has the original flag, and the historian Samuel Eliot Morison said the flag flown at Pearl Harbor in 1941 was the real one." },
        { note: "The dates: in Russia, which used the Julian calendar, the battle was on 14 and 15 May." },
        { note: "The numbers: Russian losses vary between sources. Julian Corbett's account, from 1915, gives 4,830 dead and 5,907 prisoners. Wikipedia gives 5,045 dead and 6,016 prisoners. The number of ships also changes depending on whether auxiliaries are counted." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  marne: {
    war: "First World War: the 1914 campaign on the Western Front",
    hook: "In September 1914, the Germans were at the gates of Paris, and the quick war they had planned slipped out of their hands.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In August 1914, Germany went into the war with a plan to defeat France in about six weeks and only then face Russia. It was the [[Schlieffen Plan|plano Schlieffen]]: cross Belgium with the right wing of the army, sweep through northern France and encircle the French army. The chief of the [[general staff|estado-maior]], Helmuth von Moltke, had tinkered with the plan, but kept the idea. The invasion of Belgium brought Great Britain into the war.",
        "France had a plan of its own, [[Plan XVII|plano XVII]], which expected the main German attack in Alsace and Lorraine. General Joffre went on believing this even after General Lanrezac warned that the Germans were coming through Belgium in great numbers. In August came the defeats at the Frontiers and at Mons.",
        { h: "The Great Retreat" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Aug 1914", "The Germans invade Belgium and northern France and win the battles of the Frontiers and of Mons."],
          ["24 August", "Joffre abandons Plan XVII. The French armies and the [[British Expeditionary Force|força expedicionária britânica]] begin to fall back."],
          ["3 September", "Kluck, commander of the German 1st Army, crosses the Marne river east of Paris."],
          ["5 to 9 September", "Battle of the Ourcq, northeast of Paris."],
          ["7 and 8 September", "The taxis of Paris carry troops to the front."],
          ["9 September", "Lieutenant Colonel Hentsch tells Kluck's staff that a retreat is necessary, and the German 1st Army pulls back."],
          ["14 September", "The emperor forces Moltke to resign."]
        ]},
        "The French and British armies fell back some 300 km, pursued by the German 1st, 2nd and 3rd Armies. Joffre used that time to strengthen his army: he moved troops by train from the armies in the east, brought in reservists and formed the 6th Army, northeast of Paris, under General Maunoury, with the support of the city's military governor, Gallieni. The 9th Army went to Ferdinand Foch.",
        "On the German side, the plan was wearing the army down. Moltke sent two corps east and two others to encircle Antwerp and Maubeuge. The 1st and 2nd Armies, which totaled 580,000 men on 1 August, had about 372,000 on the Marne. Kluck had run ahead of his supplies, and his soldiers were exhausted. Moltke had also ordered him to pass around Paris to the north, but Kluck ignored the idea of the order, turned southeast and crossed the Marne on 3 September, 60 km east of Paris."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Joseph Joffre, French commander" },
        "He was wrong to expect the main attack in Alsace and Lorraine. But afterward he fell back 300 km without letting the army fall apart, used the railways to move troops from one side to the other, dismissed many generals who could not cope and waited for the right moment to turn. Historians praise this patience. In his memoirs, he credited Franchet d'Espèrey with the main blow.",
        { h: "Joseph Gallieni, military governor of Paris" },
        "He commanded the defense of Paris and had fresh troops at hand. He requisitioned the city's taxis to carry reinforcements to the front, and the story became a symbol of the battle.",
        { h: "Helmuth von Moltke the Younger, chief of the German general staff" },
        "He stayed in Luxembourg, far from his armies, and lost contact with them. To find out what was happening, he sent Lieutenant Colonel Richard Hentsch to visit the commanders. Hentsch, according to some accounts, had verbal authority to order retreats.",
        { h: "Kluck and Bülow, German commanders" },
        "Alexander von Kluck (1st Army) and Karl von Bülow (2nd Army) commanded the German right wing. Kluck turned too early and opened the hole in the middle of the line. Bülow, with his army reduced to less than two thirds of what it had been at the start, told Hentsch that his soldiers were 'ashes'.",
        { h: "Franchet d'Espèrey and Foch" },
        "Franchet d'Espèrey took over the 5th Army on 3 September, in place of Lanrezac, and was frustrated by the slowness of the British in advancing through the gap. Foch commanded the 9th Army, in the center, and held back the German attack in the marshes of Saint-Gond."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Allies had about 1,080,000 men on the eve of the battle: some 980,000 French and 100,000 British, with about 3,000 guns. The Germans had between 750,000 and 900,000, with 3,300 guns, depending on the source. France had quickly reinforced the front against the German right wing: from 17.5 divisions on 23 August to 41 on 6 September, more than 700,000 men.",
        { h: "The battle in moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the Ourcq. Maunoury's 6th Army advanced northeast of Paris and met Hans von Gronau's German IV Reserve Corps, with some 24,000 men, which held the French for about a day. Kluck, warned of the danger on his flank, turned his army west and marched about 130 km in two days. His counterattack stopped the French from crossing the Ourcq and reaching the German rear.",
        "Second, the taxis. On 7 and 8 September, Gallieni requisitioned about 600 taxis to carry soldiers to Nanteuil-le-Haudouin, some 50 km away. The French Treasury paid 70,012 francs in fares. About 6,000 soldiers arrived by train, truck and taxi, but the sources do not say how many came by taxi alone. Historians disagree about the military value of the story: Hew Strachan says they were 'less than legend', and Holger Herwig considers them militarily insignificant. The effect on French morale, however, is accepted by all.",
        "Third, the gap. Kluck's turn opened a gap of about 50 km between his army and Bülow's. A French airplane discovered it, and the British force and the French 5th Army went in through it. The British advanced slowly: only some 40 km in three days, even with a ten-to-one advantage over the Germans in the gap. This irritated Franchet d'Espèrey. The 5th Army crossed the Petit Morin on 8 September and the Marne the next day.",
        "Fourth, Saint-Gond. Bülow's 2nd Army, after losing many men, fell back 15 km to the Petit Morin: it had dropped from 260,000 to about 154,000 men. Hausen's 3rd Army, with 82,000, attacked Foch's 9th Army in the marshes of Saint-Gond on the night of 8 September, without artillery preparation. It pushed Foch back 13 km and stopped, with about 11,000 casualties.",
        "Fifth, Hentsch's order. On the night of 8 September, at Bülow's headquarters, Hentsch heard that the army was in 'ashes' and that Kluck was to blame. Bülow ordered a 20 km retreat. On 9 September, at 11:30 a.m., Hentsch arrived at Kluck's headquarters and told his chief of staff, Hermann von Kuhl, that a general retreat was necessary. Kluck had to obey, and his order cited Moltke's general staff.",
        "Sixth, the general retreat. On 11 September, Moltke visited the 3rd, 4th and 5th Armies and ordered all of them to fall back to the Aisne river. The Germans stopped after about 65 km, north of the river. The Allies followed them slowly, at some 19 km a day. The frontal attacks of 15 and 16 September were repelled, and the Germans dug [[trenches|trincheira]] on the heights of the Aisne."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The German plan for a six-week war failed. On 14 September, the emperor dismissed Moltke, officially for 'illness', and put Erich von Falkenhayn in his place. Between September and October, the two sides tried to outflank each other's northern flank, in a sequence of fights called the Race to the Sea. When it ended, the front was frozen, from Flanders to Switzerland. It stayed that way for about four years.",
        "Losses are hard to count, because the battle blends into the Great Retreat and the Aisne. For the French, there are estimates of about 250,000 casualties, but another source records 213,445 for the whole month of September (18,073 dead and 83,409 missing). The British had 12,733 killed and wounded, according to Ian Sumner. For the Germans, estimates run from 67,700, in the five armies from Verdun to Paris alone (Herwig), to about 298,000. The French captured 11,717 Germans, 30 guns and 100 machine guns, and the British took 3,500 prisoners.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Herwig calls it the most important land battle of the 20th century, and John Tierney Jr. the most important in history. Richard Brooks argues that Joffre's success ruined the Schlieffen Plan and condemned Germany to a two-front war. But many historians call it a partial victory: John Terraine said it lacks the traditional look of a victory, but was decisive; Barbara Tuchman and Robert Doughty think the chance of an even bigger victory slipped away; and Ian Sumner calls it a victory with flaws.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The taxis: the episode is the most famous of the battle, but it is more symbol than strategy. Gallieni, so the story goes, admitted that some people had exaggerated its importance a little." },
        { note: "Hentsch: historians argue about how much his mission caused the retreat and how much the German armies were already exhausted and separated by gaps." },
        { note: "The sentence attributed to Moltke on being told of the failure ('we have lost the war') has uncertain authorship." },
        { note: "The dates: Wikipedia gives 5 to 12 September, counting the start of the fighting on the Ourcq. Many books give 6 to 12 September." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  tannenberg: {
    war: "First World War: the Russian invasion of East Prussia (1914)",
    hook: "The Germans built their victory partly out of the radio messages the Russians themselves sent without a code.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Under the [[Schlieffen Plan|plano Schlieffen]], Germany sent almost all of its army against France. In East Prussia, in the east, there remained the 8th Army, with only about 10% of German forces, according to the historian Prit Buttar. Russia was expected to take weeks to mobilize.",
        "It took less. Russian mobilization began in secret on 25 July, before the tsar announced it. On 17 August, Paul von Rennenkampf's 1st Army crossed the border, earlier than the Germans expected. In the south, Alexander Samsonov's 2nd Army marched into East Prussia. Russian logistics were weak: few railways, sandy roads and a lot of cavalry to feed.",
        { h: "From panic to trap" },
        { timeline: [
          ["17 Aug 1914", "Rennenkampf invades East Prussia from the east."],
          ["20 August", "Battle of Gumbinnen. The German general Prittwitz telephones the supreme command in a panic and talks of retreating to the Vistula river."],
          ["22 August", "Moltke dismisses Prittwitz and puts Hindenburg and Ludendorff in his place."],
          ["24 August", "Hindenburg, Ludendorff and the officer Max Hoffmann meet General Scholtz and plan the encirclement."],
          ["26 to 30 August", "Battle of Tannenberg."],
          ["31 August", "Hindenburg informs the emperor of the victory and asks that it be called Tannenberg."]
        ]},
        "The Russian army, unlike the Germans, had a weakness in communications. The Russians had broken German ciphers in the past, but kept on using their own codes, and Samsonov, who had no codebook, sent orders by radio without a code. The Germans heard them."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Paul von Hindenburg and Erich Ludendorff" },
        "Hindenburg was 66 and retired when he was called back. Ludendorff had made his name at the siege of Liège, in Belgium, and became his chief of staff. Hindenburg later said the victory was 'ours', but Ludendorff wrote in his memoirs that the encirclement was his idea. Walter Elze recorded that Hindenburg later admitted Ludendorff had panicked on the night of 26 August. Most historians give Ludendorff the credit for the conduct of the battle.",
        { h: "Max Hoffmann, staff officer" },
        "He was a key piece in the planning and came to be ignored by the press. Later, he took to guiding visits to the battlefield and also claimed the choice of the name Tannenberg.",
        { h: "Alexander Samsonov, commander of the Russian 2nd Army" },
        "His army advanced too fast, far from its supplies, and its corps spread out. According to the sources, he quarreled with General Zhilinsky, chief of the northwest front, over the direction of the advance, and Zhilinsky gave way, but demanded that the VI Corps be diverted to Bischofsburg. On seeing his army destroyed, he vanished into the forest and killed himself.",
        { h: "Paul von Rennenkampf, commander of the Russian 1st Army" },
        "After Gumbinnen, he decided to stop and regroup. German airplanes saw him marching toward the German rear on 28 August, but he arrived too late to help Samsonov. The story that he hated Samsonov because of a fistfight at Mukden station in 1905 is repeated in many books, but most historians consider it a legend with no proof."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The German 8th Army had about 150,000 men, 294 [[machine guns|metralhadora]] and 728 guns and howitzers. The main corps were the I (Hermann von François), the XVII (August von Mackensen), the I Reserve (Otto von Below) and the XX (Friedrich von Scholtz). The Russian 2nd Army had between 123,000 and 180,000 men, 384 machine guns and 612 guns, depending on the source, spread over several [[army corps|corpo de exército]]: the I, the VI, the XIII, the XV and the XXIII.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The battle was near Allenstein (today Olsztyn), amid forests and [[lakes|lagos da masúria]], with Hohenstein, Usdau, Soldau and Neidenburg as landmarks. The name comes from the village of Tannenberg, some 30 km from Allenstein. The spot where the Russians were surrounded is near Frogenau.",
        { h: "The battle in moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the plan. On 22 and 23 August, Samsonov pushed the Germans back, and the XX Corps fell back under pressure. On the 24th, the Germans planned the encirclement based on [[intercepted messages|mensagem em claro]] and captured maps. Samsonov, unaware, kept spreading out his corps.",
        "Second, the Russian right flank. Between 26 and 27 August, the Germans defeated the Russian VI Corps, which fled. It was Samsonov's right tip.",
        "Third, the left tip. François waited for his artillery to arrive, attacked Usdau and took it on 27 August. According to some accounts, Hindenburg and Ludendorff ordered him to turn north and he ignored the order. That night, Ludendorff ordered the encirclement.",
        "Fourth, the squeeze. On 28 August, François took Soldau and advanced on Neidenburg, behind the Russians. Below and Mackensen closed in from the east. German airplanes warned that Rennenkampf was marching toward their rear, but Hindenburg kept to the plan.",
        "Fifth, the ring. On 29 August, François's cavalry reached Willenberg, and Mackensen reached Jedwabno. The encirclement was complete. The Russians in the center were destroyed at Frogenau. On the 30th, attempts to break the ring failed.",
        "Sixth, the end. On the night of 30 August, Samsonov killed himself in the forest. His body was found the following year."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Hindenburg said he had captured about 92,000 Russians, a figure the official German archive (the Reichsarchiv) confirmed, and another 50,000 dead. Other historians think the numbers too high, because only about half of the Russian army was surrounded. One analysis counted 120,219 Russian losses in all (5,522 dead, 12,326 wounded and 75,435 missing), and Alexei Oleynikov says they did not exceed 80,000. The Germans lost about 12,000 men by the official figures (1,888 dead, 6,579 wounded and 4,588 missing), but other estimates run as high as 30,000. It took 60 trains to carry away the captured material.",
        "About a week later, the reinforced 8th Army attacked Rennenkampf in the First Battle of the Masurian Lakes, and the Russian 1st Army fell back across the border. Rennenkampf escaped condemnation, but retired afterward. Zhilinsky was removed.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Russia lost an entire army in the first weeks of the war, and Germany gained two heroes, Hindenburg and Ludendorff, who would later dominate the country. David Stevenson calls it a great victory, but not a decisive one: Germany was still fighting on two fronts, and the success led to overconfidence in the later operations at Warsaw and Łódź. The British general Edmund Ironside called it the greatest defeat suffered by either side in the whole war.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The name: the battle was fought near Allenstein, some 30 km from the village of Tannenberg. Hindenburg chose the name to avenge the Teutonic Knights, defeated at nearby Grunwald in 1410." },
        { note: "The numbers: both sides are uncertain. The Russian total ranges from 123,000 to 180,000 men for the 2nd Army, and the number of prisoners from 92,000 to a good deal less." },
        { note: "Credit: Hindenburg, Ludendorff and Hoffmann each claimed the merit, each in his own way." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  galipoli: {
    war: "First World War: the Dardanelles campaign (1915–1916)",
    hook: "An idea of Churchill's to shorten the war became eight months of trenches, disease and death on a narrow peninsula.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "The Ottoman Empire entered the First World War on 31 October 1914, after two warships that had been German, the Yavuz and the Midilli, bombarded Russian ports on the Black Sea. In late November, Winston Churchill, then First Lord of the Admiralty, proposed attacking the [[Dardanelles strait|dardanelos]] from the sea. The idea was to open the strait, bombard Constantinople, the Ottoman capital, and cut it off from the empire's Asian lands. The plan rested on wrong information about the size of the Ottoman forces.",
        { h: "From sea to land" },
        { timeline: [
          ["31 Oct 1914", "The Ottoman Empire enters the war on Germany's side."],
          ["19 Feb 1915", "The bombardment of the Dardanelles forts begins."],
          ["18 March", "Eighteen Allied battleships attack the narrowest point of the strait. Three sink and others are damaged. The attempt by sea ends."],
          ["25 April", "Allied troops land at several points on the Gallipoli peninsula."],
          ["6 to 10 August", "New offensive, with a landing at Suvla and attacks on the Sari Bair ridge."],
          ["19 and 20 December", "Evacuation of Anzac and Suvla."],
          ["8 and 9 Jan 1916", "Evacuation of Helles, the last sector."]
        ]},
        "On 18 March, 18 Allied battleships attacked the narrowest point of the channel, where it is about 1.6 km wide. The French Bouvet hit a [[naval mine|mina naval]] and capsized in two minutes, with 75 survivors out of 718 men. The British Irresistible sank, and the Ocean was abandoned after being hit and striking a mine. The mines were part of a new line laid ten days earlier by the Ottoman ship Nusret. Admiral de Robeck sent the signal to withdraw. The ships did not try again, and the plan became to land troops."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Winston Churchill, First Lord of the Admiralty" },
        "He was the great champion of the plan. When it went wrong, he paid the price: in May, Admiral Fisher, head of the Navy, resigned after quarreling with Churchill, and Churchill was removed from the Admiralty as a condition for the Conservatives to join the government. He resigned from the cabinet in November 1915.",
        { h: "Ian Hamilton, British commander" },
        "Kitchener chose him to command the Allied force, of about 78,000 men. The Dardanelles Commission, which investigated the campaign, later concluded that he was too optimistic from the start.",
        { h: "Otto Liman von Sanders, German commander of the Ottoman 5th Army" },
        "He was a German general at the head of an army of Turkish conscripts. He thought the landing would be at Besika Bay, far from there, and so kept his troops on the move, with the 19th Division near Boghali as a general reserve.",
        { h: "Mustafa Kemal, Ottoman lieutenant colonel" },
        "He commanded the 19th Division. He correctly foresaw that the Allies would land at Helles and Gaba Tepe, and thought the troops were too spread out to repel a landing on the beach. It was his performance at Gallipoli that made him known. Later he founded, and was the first president of, the Republic of Turkey, under the name Atatürk."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "According to Wikipedia, the Allies sent between 489,000 and 550,000 men over the course of the campaign: some 345,000 British, including Irish, Indians and Newfoundlanders, 79,000 French, about 50,000 Australians and 15,000 New Zealanders. The Ottoman forces numbered 250,000 to 315,500, with about 700 Germans. On the front line, at the start of April, the Ottomans had 60,000 to 62,077 men. The Allied delay gave the Ottomans about four weeks to ring the beaches with wire, dig trenches and improvise mines.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Gallipoli is a long peninsula, with steep hills right above the beaches. Whoever was on the heights saw everything and fired from above. Whoever landed was pinned on a narrow strip of sand, under fire, and the troops fell into disorder in the ravines.",
        { h: "25 April: the landing" },
        "The landing was postponed from 23 to 25 April because of the weather. At Anzac, the Australian and New Zealand soldiers landed about 2 km north of the planned spot, because of currents or a navigation error. The steep terrain broke up the units. The Ottomans counterattacked in mid-morning. Generals Bridges and Godley recommended reembarking, but Hamilton ordered them to dig trenches. The Australians ended up holding a perimeter from Walker's Ridge to Shell Green.",
        "At Helles, on the tip of the peninsula, the 29th Division landed with enormous losses on V and W beaches, where the ships hesitated to come close to give support. Of the first 200 soldiers who left the ship River Clyde, only 21 reached the beach. The Lancashires lost 600 of 1,000 men on W beach. Of the 1,012 soldiers of the Dublin Fusiliers who landed, only 11 would come out of the campaign without a scratch.",
        "That morning, Mustafa Kemal gave an order to the 57th Regiment, according to Wikipedia: 'I do not order you to attack, I order you to die. In the time that passes until we die, other troops and other commanders can take our place.' The regiment was almost destroyed: every man was killed or wounded.",
        { h: "The August offensive" },
        "After months of trenches, the British tried to break the line in August. On the night of 6 August, they landed at Suvla, against little resistance. But General Stopford limited his objectives and did not advance inland, and almost only the beach was taken. The Ottomans occupied the Anafarta hills and surrounded the landing. On 7 August, at The Nek, the artillery barrage stopped seven minutes early and warned the defenders, and the attack was a costly failure. The New Zealanders held Chunuk Bair for two days, but an Ottoman counterattack led by Kemal, on 10 August, retook the heights. At Lone Pine, a diversionary attack took the main Ottoman trench line.",
        { h: "The withdrawal" },
        "The evacuation was raised on 11 October 1915. Hamilton was recalled to London in October, and Monro took over. Kitchener visited the area in November and agreed, and the cabinet confirmed the withdrawal in early December. They expected up to 30,000 casualties. The last soldiers from Anzac and Suvla left before dawn on 20 December. To fool the Ottomans, they used tricks such as William Scurry's automatic rifle, fired by dripping water. The Australians had no casualties on the last night. At Helles, the decision was taken on 28 December, and the last soldiers left around 4 a.m. on 8 January, with the Newfoundlanders in the rear guard until the 9th. In all, they withdrew 35,268 soldiers, 3,689 horses and mules, 127 guns and 328 vehicles."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The campaign ended in January 1916 with an Ottoman victory. According to Wikipedia, the Allies had 252,000 to 300,000 casualties, with 43,000 to 61,398 dead, and lost 7 battleships and 8 submarines. The Ottomans had 255,268 to 289,160 casualties, with 56,938 to 87,000 dead. The ranges vary widely because counting the sick, the wounded and the dead is difficult.",
        "Disease was an enemy in its own right: 145,154 British soldiers (not counting those from the dominions and India) fell ill, and 3,778 died. Dysentery infected 29,728 men, and the cold caused 6,602 hospital admissions for frostbite.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "For Churchill, it was a political blow. The Dardanelles Commission, set up to investigate, published its first report in 1917 and the final one in 1919. For Turkey, it was the basis of Mustafa Kemal's fame and, with it, of the Republic he founded in 1923. For Australia and New Zealand, the campaign is seen by many as the start of national consciousness. 25 April, Anzac Day, is the most important day for remembering soldiers in both countries, more than Remembrance Day, in November.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Kemal's sentence: the order appears in different wordings ('I do not order you to attack', 'I do not order you to fight'), and the exact version depends on the translation from the Turkish." },
        { note: "The numbers: both the troop totals and the death totals depend on what is counted. Wikipedia gives the ranges above, and the number of wounded and sick is even more uncertain." },
        { note: "The evacuation: it was feared it would cost up to 30,000 casualties, and the Allies had far fewer. The Australians had no casualties on the last night at Anzac." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  verdun: {
    war: "First World War, Western Front (1916)",
    hook: "A city that Germany perhaps did not even want to take cost, in ten months, about 700,000 dead and wounded.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "At the end of 1915, the war was stuck in the trenches. General Erich von Falkenhayn, chief of the German general staff, believed, according to his memoirs written after the war, that France could only be beaten if it lost so many men that it could no longer fight, rather than in one great victory. According to the emperor and Gerhard Tappen, he saw Verdun as the best chance to do this. Hence the phrase that stuck, 'bleeding white', which historians debate: it comes from texts written after the war.",
        "Verdun lay in a [[salient|saliente]] of the French line, surrounded by a ring of [[forts|forte]]. Joffre thought the forts too old against heavy German artillery. In 1915, he took 237 guns and about 647 tons of ammunition out of Verdun and left the fort garrisons reduced to a few men. The railways to the city had been cut in 1914 and in mid-1915, and only a light railway was left. Fort Douaumont was almost without soldiers: the garrison was a crew of about 68 technicians, under a non-commissioned officer. Colonel Driant criticized the disarming of Verdun, and Joffre ignored him.",
        { timeline: [
          ["Late 1915", "Falkenhayn decides to attack Verdun to wear France down."],
          ["21 Feb 1916", "Opening bombardment and start of the German attack."],
          ["25 February", "A small German party enters Fort Douaumont, almost without defenders."],
          ["Late Feb 1916", "Pétain takes over the defense of Verdun."],
          ["March to June", "Fighting at Mort-Homme and Côte 304. Fort Vaux falls in June."],
          ["July", "The Battle of the Somme draws resources from both sides, and the German offensive at Verdun is limited."],
          ["24 Oct and 2 Nov", "French counterattacks retake Douaumont and Vaux."],
          ["18 December", "End of the battle, by the usual date."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Erich von Falkenhayn, chief of the German general staff" },
        "His aim is debated. Falkenhayn wanted constant pressure, but the German 5th Army wanted to take Verdun quickly. The two wanted different things, and the ambiguity was costly.",
        { h: "Philippe Pétain, French commander" },
        "He took over the defense of Verdun at the end of February. Later he was promoted to commander of the Army Group Center. He set up the system of troop rotation: divisions left the line after a few weeks, and others took their place. In this way, a large part of the French army passed through Verdun.",
        { h: "Robert Nivelle" },
        "He took command of the 2nd Army at Verdun when Pétain moved up. On 23 June 1916, he issued the order of the day 'You will not let them pass, comrades', which gave rise to the phrase 'They shall not pass'."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Germans attacked with the 5th Army, and about 50 divisions passed through Verdun over the course of the battle, according to Wikipedia. On the French side, the infobox speaks of 75 divisions. At the start, the sector had 14.5 divisions on 26 February and 20.5 on 6 March.",
        { h: "21 February: the bombardment" },
        "A mass of 808 guns fired for ten hours, which added up to about 1,000,000 shells on a front of some 30 km by 5 km. In all, the front had 1,201 guns concentrated. The 420 mm guns fired on the forts and the city. On 25 February, about 100 Germans entered Fort Douaumont through an undefended ditch and captured the reduced garrison.",
        { h: "The Sacred Way" },
        "The only railway serving Verdun was light and small, and the bulk of supply had to go by truck, along the 72 km road between Bar-le-Duc and Verdun. It had been widened to 7 meters in 1915, to take two lanes of trucks. In the first phase of the crisis, from 21 February to 22 March, about 600 trucks a day carried 48,000 tons of ammunition, 6,400 of other materiel and 263,000 men. After March, according to tradition, a truck passed every 14 seconds. There were 3,500 trucks and 800 ambulances, and a traffic control unit had 300 officers and 8,500 men. The road was given the name Sacred Way. A narrow-gauge railway, the Meusien, ran alongside and carried 1,800 tons a day.",
        { h: "The fight for the west bank" },
        "The Germans took parts of Mort-Homme on 14 March and the north slope of Côte 304 on 4 May. On 7 June, the 574 survivors of Fort Vaux surrendered. By late June, the Germans had advanced some 5 km toward the citadel of Verdun. Between 23 June and 17 August, Fleury changed hands 16 times.",
        "In July, the German attack was limited, to save troops and ammunition for the Somme, and the French 10th Army was transferred there. From autumn, the French counterattacked. On 24 October, they retook Fort Douaumont and took about 6,000 prisoners. On 2 November, Fort Vaux came back into French hands. The battle ended on 18 December, by the usual date."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "According to Heer and Naumann (2000), the French had 377,231 casualties and the Germans 337,000, for a total of 714,231, more than 70,000 a month. Philpott (2014) arrives at about 714,000 in all. The Wikipedia infobox gives wider ranges: from 336,000 to 355,000 for Germany, and from 379,000 to 400,000 for France. The line stayed practically where it had been in February.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It is remembered as one of the longest battles of the war and a symbol of French sacrifice. For Germany, the offensive failed at what mattered most: it did not knock France out of the war and cost the German army almost as many men as the French. The phrase 'They shall not pass' became a motto of French resistance.",
        "At Douaumont, a large ossuary holds the remains of unidentified soldiers, and several villages in the region were destroyed and never rebuilt.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "'Bleeding white': the idea that Falkenhayn wanted to destroy the French army comes from memoirs written after the war. Historians debate whether it was really the plan or a justification after the failure." },
        { note: "The Sacred Way: the phrase 'a truck every 14 seconds' is repeated in many texts, but with no source cited." },
        { note: "The numbers: casualties vary widely between sources, especially German dead (from 71,000 to 143,000, according to the Wikipedia infobox)." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  somme: {
    war: "First World War, Western Front (1916)",
    hook: "On the first day, the British army lost 57,470 men: the worst day in its history.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In December 1915, at the Chantilly conference, the Allies agreed on simultaneous attacks in 1916 on the eastern, Italian and western fronts. Douglas Haig, who replaced John French in the British command that same month, preferred to attack in Flanders. In February 1916, an offensive on the Somme was agreed, with France in the main role and the British in support.",
        "The German attack on Verdun, on 21 February, changed everything. France had to divert divisions, and its share on the Somme fell to 13 divisions of the 6th Army, against 20 British. By 31 May, the plan had already become a limited offensive, to relieve Verdun and wear the Germans down.",
        { timeline: [
          ["Dec 1915", "Chantilly conference. Haig takes over the British command."],
          ["21 Feb 1916", "The Germans attack Verdun."],
          ["24 June", "The preparatory bombardment begins."],
          ["1 July", "First day of the battle."],
          ["14 July", "Battle of Bazentin Ridge."],
          ["15 September", "Tanks are used for the first time."],
          ["13 to 18 November", "Last major British operation, the Battle of the Ancre."]
        ]},
        "General Rawlinson commanded the British 4th Army, and General Gough the Reserve Army. On the German side, Fritz von Below's 2nd Army held the region. Falkenhayn sent only four divisions as reinforcement and kept eight in reserve. The Germans already knew of the attack: a message from Rawlinson to the troops was one of the clues, and research published in 2016 says two disgruntled soldiers handed over the date and place weeks earlier."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The leading figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Douglas Haig, British commander" },
        "He preferred to attack in Flanders, but accepted the Somme. Churchill criticized the conduct of the battle in August 1916, and Lloyd George condemned it in his memoirs. Historians such as Terraine, Sheffield and Duffy argue that Britain had no strategic alternative in 1916.",
        { h: "Henry Rawlinson, commander of the 4th Army" },
        "He commanded the 4th Army, which took the brunt of the first day's attack. The message he sent to the troops was one of the clues that alerted the Germans.",
        { h: "Fritz von Below, commander of the German 2nd Army" },
        "He held the Somme region. Falkenhayn sent him only four divisions as reinforcement and kept eight in reserve."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The defenses and the bombardment" },
        "The Germans had built three lines of defense. The dugouts were deepened to 20 to 30 feet (6 to 9 meters), and each housed up to 25 men. The [[barbed wire|arame farpado]] formed two wide belts. The front trenches, however, lay on a very visible chalk slope and were full of men, exposed to the bombardment.",
        "The preparatory bombardment began on 24 June. The plan called for five days. More than 1.7 million shells were fired, according to the sources. The idea was to destroy the barbed wire and the trenches. But many Germans waited in the deep dugouts, and the British National Army Museum says that up to 30% of the shells did not explode. The Allied [[artillery|artilharia]] was large, but it was not enough.",
        { h: "1 July: the first day" },
        "Zero hour was at 7:30. Nineteen [[underground mines|mina subterrânea]] were blown along the line, the one at Hawthorn Ridge, with about 18 tons, at 7:20, ten minutes early, and the one at Lochnagar, the largest of the war on the Western Front, around 7:28 (the sources disagree on the exact time). Five French divisions attacked east of the river, eleven British north of the river up to Serre, and two of the 3rd Army made a diversionary action at Gommecourt.",
        "The French and the British south of the Albert-Bapaume road had good success. North of the road, the British attack was a disaster. In a single day, the British 4th Army had 57,470 casualties, with 19,240 dead: the worst day in the history of the British army. The French had about 1,590 casualties, and the German 2nd Army about 10,000 to 12,000. The Newfoundland Regiment, with about 801 men at Beaumont-Hamel, had only 68 who answered the roll call at the end of the day, according to the Canadian Encyclopedia. In Northern Ireland, the battle is remembered for the 36th (Ulster) Division.",
        "Haig abandoned the attack in the north to reinforce the success in the south, against Joffre's wishes.",
        { h: "Bazentin Ridge and after" },
        "On 14 July, four divisions attacked at 3:25 a.m., behind five minutes of heavy bombardment and a [[creeping barrage|barragem rolante]]. Most objectives were taken, but the attack was not exploited because of communication failures. The South African brigade held Delville Wood from 15 to 20 July and lost 2,536 men. Between 23 July and 7 August, the Australian 1st Division took Pozières.",
        { h: "The tanks" },
        "On 15 September, in the Battle of Flers-Courcelette, the [[tanks|tanque]], the Canadian Corps and the New Zealand Division made their debut. Forty-nine Mark I tanks were set aside, 32 reached the start line and about 15 went into action. The line advanced between 2,500 and 3,500 yards, without breaking through. Between 26 and 28 September came the Battle of Thiepval Ridge. The autumn operations, with rain and German counterattacks, moved slowly, and the battle ended with the Ancre, between 13 and 18 November."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Allies penetrated about 10 km on most of the front: the biggest advance since the Marne, in 1914. But they did not take Péronne or Bapaume, and the operational objectives were not reached. More than three million men fought, and more than a million died or were wounded. British casualties were between 419,654 and 432,000, and French about 200,000. German ones vary a great deal: 237,159 according to the official German archive, for the 69 divisions on the battlefield, up to 465,181 to 500,000 and perhaps 600,000 by other counts. Wikipedia notes that Allied estimates during the war exaggerated German losses. The French took 41,605 prisoners, and the British 31,396.",
        "In March 1917, the Germans fell back about 40 km, to the [[Hindenburg Line|linha hindenburg]], in Operation Alberich.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "In the 1930s, the dominant view was one of 'mud, blood and futility'. Peter Barton, in 2016, suggested calling it a German defensive victory. Philpott argues that the German army was exhausted at the end of 1916, a collapse that began on the Somme. According to Wikipedia, it was where the British learned to conduct mass industrial war: a transport system much larger than expected was needed, which moved 20,000 tons a day on a 12-mile front during attacks.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The Newfoundland Regiment received the title 'Royal' on 28 November 1917, in recognition of its effort on the first day. In Newfoundland, 1 July is remembered at 11 a.m. on the Sunday closest to the date." },
        { note: "In 2016, on the centenary, there were two minutes of silence and an event in which 1,400 actors portrayed soldiers killed on the first day." },
        { note: "The numbers: German losses are highly disputed. The range runs from 237,159 to 600,000, and the Allied estimates made during the war were exaggerated." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

